# Phase 1: System Design

Project: IoT-Based Smart Asset Tracking and Unauthorized Movement Detection System

This is the detailed Phase 1 document you can share with your project partner so both of you understand what we are building before starting the code.

## 1. Problem Statement

In warehouses, storage rooms, and malls, assets may be misplaced, moved to the wrong location, or removed without authorization. Manual checking makes it difficult to identify these problems quickly.

Our project aims to develop an IoT-based system that tracks assets, checks their expected and detected locations, and identifies suspicious movement or unauthorized exits at the main gate.

## 2. Proposed Solution

Each asset will have a unique ID associated with a tracking tag. Tracking devices installed in different zones will detect assets and send their information to a backend system.

The backend will compare the detected location with the expected location and check whether the asset is authorized to leave.

The system will display asset statuses and generate alerts when suspicious activity is detected.

## 3. Objectives

- Track assets across predefined zones.
- Detect misplaced assets.
- Identify assets that have not been detected within the defined checking period.
- Verify authorization when an asset reaches the main gate.
- Generate alerts for unauthorized exits.
- Display asset information and event history on a dashboard.

## 4. System Architecture

Asset Tracking Tag

RFID or BLE

ESP32

Reads or detects tag information

Wi-Fi + MQTT

Transfers tracking events

Python Backend

Processes locations and authorization

SQLite

Stores data

Streamlit

Shows results

Note: RFID is suitable for checkpoint detection; BLE can support zone-level proximity tracking. We will finalize one technology before implementing the tracking code.

## 5. Functional Requirements

| Requirement             | Description                                      |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Asset registration      | Store each asset's ID and name                   |
| Location monitoring     | Compare expected and detected zones              |
| Misplacement detection  | Flag an asset detected in the wrong zone         |
| Missing-asset detection | Flag an asset not detected within a defined time |
| Exit verification       | Check authorization at the main gate             |
| Alert generation        | Notify users about suspicious events             |
| Event history           | Store timestamps, locations, and statuses        |
| Dashboard               | Display assets, alerts, and current status       |

## 6. Example of Expected Output

| Asset ID | Expected Zone | Detected Location | Status            |
| -------- | ------------- | ----------------- | ----------------- |
| A001     | SHELF_01      | SHELF_01          | NORMAL            |
| A002     | SHELF_01      | SHELF_03          | MISPLACED         |
| A003     | SHELF_02      | Not detected      | MISSING           |
| A004     | SHELF_02      | MAIN_GATE         | AUTHORIZED EXIT   |
| A005     | SHELF_03      | MAIN_GATE         | UNAUTHORIZED EXIT |

These are example results for testing. A real deployment will depend on actual tag readings, reader placement, and the missing-item timeout.

Important: A missing asset is not automatically classified as stolen. An unauthorized exit is identified when the asset is detected at the gate without valid authorization.

## 7. Database Design

We will use SQLite with three main tables.

`assets`

Stores asset details:

- `asset_id`
- `asset_name`
- `expected_zone`

`events`

Stores tracking history:

- `event_id`
- `asset_id`
- `event_type`
- `location`
- `timestamp`
- `status`

`authorization`

Stores exit permission:

- `asset_id`
- `authorized_exit`

